::ENG2_U03_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "We cross this bridge to reach work. We cross this bridge to visit family." Question: What rhetorical choice is used?{
=A. Repetition#Correct. The phrase "We cross this bridge" repeats for emphasis.
~B. Statistic#Incorrect. No numbers appear.
~C. Dialogue#Incorrect. No conversation appears.
~D. Definition#Incorrect. The passage does not define a term.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "Shall we wait until the boards fail beneath us, or shall we act while repair is still possible?" Question: What is the effect of this rhetorical question?{
~A. It asks for a literal answer on paper.#Incorrect. It is used to make a point.
=B. It makes action now seem responsible and delay seem risky.#Correct. The question pushes the audience toward repair.
~C. It makes bridge repair sound unimportant.#Incorrect. The question creates urgency.
~D. It changes the subject to schools.#Incorrect. It stays on bridge repair.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "A private well may serve one house, but a public well serves the whole road." Question: Which rhetorical choice is used?{
~A. Dialogue#Incorrect. No one speaks back and forth.
~B. Repetition only#Incorrect. The main choice is contrast.
=C. Contrast#Correct. The sentence contrasts private and public benefit.
~D. Rhyme#Incorrect. The effect is not based on rhyme.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "Open the gates for the child... Open the gates for the worker... Open the gates for the elder..." Question: What is the effect of the repetition?{
~A. It suggests only one person needs the park.#Incorrect. Several people are named.
~B. It hides the author's purpose.#Incorrect. It makes the purpose clear.
~C. It proves the park should close.#Incorrect. It supports opening the park.
=D. It emphasizes that many community members benefit.#Correct. The repeated phrase connects different groups to the same action.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "Survival is not a privilege for the fortunate; it is a duty shared by neighbors." Question: What appeal is used?{
=A. Appeal to fairness and shared responsibility#Correct. The sentence contrasts privilege with shared duty.
~B. Appeal to comedy#Incorrect. The tone is serious.
~C. Appeal to silence#Incorrect. The sentence makes a public argument.
~D. Appeal to decoration#Incorrect. Decoration is not the focus.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "We need roads that carry food. We need roads that carry medicine. We need roads that carry hope." Question: What does repetition achieve?{
~A. It weakens the importance of roads.#Incorrect. It emphasizes importance.
=B. It creates rhythm and emphasizes several public needs.#Correct. The repeated phrase builds urgency.
~C. It proves roads are unnecessary.#Incorrect. The passage argues the opposite.
~D. It removes emotion from the speech.#Incorrect. "hope" adds emotion.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "Shall we build the wall while the sky is clear?" Question: Why is this rhetorical question effective?{
~A. It makes preparation seem foolish.#Incorrect. The question supports preparation.
~B. It asks about weather only.#Incorrect. Weather is part of the image, not the full point.
=C. It encourages action before danger returns.#Correct. The question frames early action as wise.
~D. It avoids audience involvement.#Incorrect. A rhetorical question pulls the audience into the reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "This is not a favor for one street; it is a safeguard for the entire town." Question: What is the effect of the contrast?{
~A. It makes the proposal seem narrow.#Incorrect. It expands the proposal to the entire town.
~B. It changes the topic to entertainment.#Incorrect. The topic remains public safety.
~C. It hides the author's claim.#Incorrect. It clarifies the claim.
=D. It frames the proposal as a broad public benefit.#Correct. The contrast moves from one street to the whole town.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "A safe bridge is not a luxury; it is a promise." Question: Which choice best explains the effect?{
=A. The contrast makes safety seem like a duty, not an optional extra.#Correct. The sentence contrasts luxury with promise.
~B. The sentence defines luxury only.#Incorrect. It uses contrast to make a claim.
~C. The sentence avoids purpose.#Incorrect. It supports persuasion.
~D. The sentence includes a statistic.#Incorrect. No number appears.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "No family should wonder whether clean water belongs only to those with deeper pockets." Question: What does "deeper pockets" suggest?{
~A. Literal pockets full of water#Incorrect. The phrase is figurative.
=B. Greater wealth or ability to pay#Correct. The phrase suggests financial privilege.
~C. A sewing lesson#Incorrect. The context is water access.
~D. A hidden map#Incorrect. The phrase is about money.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "What kind of town locks away the very ground that could gather its people together?" Question: What is the effect of the rhetorical question?{
~A. It makes closing the park seem generous.#Incorrect. It criticizes locking the park.
=B. It pressures the audience to see park closure as wrong.#Correct. The question implies a good town would open shared space.
~C. It asks for the town's exact population.#Incorrect. It is not a literal information question.
~D. It changes the topic to farming.#Incorrect. The topic remains the park.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "A shared well is a practical tool, but it is also a statement." Question: What rhetorical effect does this contrast create?{
=A. It shows the well has both practical and symbolic meaning.#Correct. The sentence expands the meaning of the well.
~B. It proves the well is useless.#Incorrect. It calls the well practical.
~C. It makes water seem imaginary.#Incorrect. The well is practical and symbolic.
~D. It avoids the author's purpose.#Incorrect. It supports the purpose.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "Open the gates for the child... the worker... the elder..." Question: Why list different people?{
=A. To show that park access benefits multiple groups#Correct. The list broadens the appeal.
~B. To prove only children need parks#Incorrect. Workers and elders are also named.
~C. To make the passage about one person#Incorrect. The list includes several people.
~D. To avoid persuasion#Incorrect. The list supports persuasion.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "We cross this bridge..." repeated three times. Question: How does repetition support author purpose?{
~A. It makes the bridge seem unused.#Incorrect. Repetition shows frequent use.
=B. It reminds the audience that the bridge is part of daily life.#Correct. The repeated phrase shows many reasons people cross.
~C. It proves the author opposes repair.#Incorrect. It supports repair.
~D. It removes emphasis.#Incorrect. Repetition creates emphasis.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "Survival is not a privilege for the fortunate." Question: What tone does this sentence create?{
~A. Casual and playful#Incorrect. The language is serious.
~B. Confused and uncertain#Incorrect. The sentence is direct.
=C. Serious and justice-focused#Correct. The sentence frames survival as a matter of fairness.
~D. Silly and unrelated#Incorrect. It is serious and relevant.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "Shall we wait... or shall we act..." Question: What choice is being used?{
=A. Rhetorical question with contrast#Correct. It asks a question that contrasts waiting with acting.
~B. A statistic with data#Incorrect. No number or data appears.
~C. Dialogue between two speakers#Incorrect. There is one speaker.
~D. A glossary definition#Incorrect. No term is defined.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "A private well may serve one house, but a public well serves the whole road." Question: How does this support purpose?{
~A. It avoids comparing ideas.#Incorrect. It compares private and public.
~B. It proves private wells should be the only option.#Incorrect. The sentence values the public well.
~C. It hides the public benefit.#Incorrect. It emphasizes it.
=D. It persuades readers that public resources help more people.#Correct. The contrast shows broader public benefit.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "We need roads that carry food... medicine... hope." Question: Which word adds emotional appeal?{
~A. roads#Incorrect. This names the topic.
=B. hope#Correct. "Hope" adds emotional meaning beyond practical transportation.
~C. that#Incorrect. This is a function word.
~D. carry#Incorrect. This verb is useful, but hope carries the strongest emotional appeal.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "A safe bridge is not a luxury; it is a promise." Question: What is the rhetorical choice?{
~A. Technical definition#Incorrect. It is rhetorical contrast.
~B. Dialogue#Incorrect. No dialogue appears.
~C. A list of dates#Incorrect. No dates appear.
=D. Contrast between luxury and promise#Correct. The sentence contrasts optional comfort with public duty.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "in this town, survival is not a privilege..." Question: How does "in this town" affect the audience?{
~A. It changes the subject to weather.#Incorrect. Weather is not discussed.
~B. It makes the issue unrelated to the audience.#Incorrect. It localizes the issue.
~C. It removes civic responsibility.#Incorrect. The sentence emphasizes shared duty.
=D. It makes the issue local and shared.#Correct. The phrase connects the argument directly to the audience's community.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "Open the gates..." repeated at the start of three sentences. Question: What is this technique called?{
=A. Repetition#Correct. The same phrase begins multiple sentences.
~B. Citation#Incorrect. No source is cited.
~C. Definition#Incorrect. No term is defined.
~D. Summary#Incorrect. It is a repeated command.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "What kind of town locks away the very ground that could gather its people together?" Question: What value does the question appeal to?{
~A. Decoration only#Incorrect. The value is community, not decoration.
~B. Private profit only#Incorrect. The question argues for public access.
~C. Silence#Incorrect. The passage calls for reflection and action.
=D. Community belonging#Correct. The phrase "gather its people together" appeals to shared community.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "not a privilege... a duty" Question: What does this contrast emphasize?{
=A. The shift from individual advantage to shared responsibility#Correct. The contrast frames survival as everyone's concern.
~B. The difference between two recipes#Incorrect. No recipes appear.
~C. The importance of silence#Incorrect. The contrast is about responsibility.
~D. The need to ignore neighbors#Incorrect. It emphasizes neighbors.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Speech Passage: "We cross this bridge to bring help when help is needed." Question: What appeal is strongest?{
~A. Appeal to confusion#Incorrect. The sentence is clear.
~B. Appeal to unrelated humor#Incorrect. The sentence is serious.
~C. Appeal to private luxury#Incorrect. The passage frames the bridge as public need.
=D. Appeal to shared safety and care#Correct. The detail reminds the audience that the bridge enables help.
}

::ENG2_U03_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.R.12] Historical Essay Passage: "A shared well is a practical tool, but it is also a statement." Question: Which explanation best analyzes effect?{
~A. The author focuses only on decoration.#Incorrect. The focus is public need and value.
~B. The author says the well has no use.#Incorrect. It is called practical.
~C. The author avoids making any claim.#Incorrect. The sentence makes a claim.
=D. The author makes the well seem both useful and morally meaningful.#Correct. The sentence expands the well from practical object to public value.
}
